[
  {
    "topicTitle": "Maven Fundamentals",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-1-section-1",
        "title": "Maven's Role in Java Build Automation and DevOps",
        "content": "Apache Maven is a build automation and project management tool widely used in Java-based software development. Its primary responsibility is to provide a consistent way to describe a project, resolve the libraries required by that project, execute build operations, run tests, and produce distributable artifacts. Instead of requiring every developer to manually coordinate compiler commands, library files, test execution, and packaging steps, Maven provides a standard build environment driven by project configuration.\n\nMaven is particularly useful when a project contains multiple modules, external dependencies, automated tests, and repeatable release requirements. A developer can use the same Maven command locally that a CI server uses to validate the application. This reduces differences between individual development machines and build agents.\n\nIn a DevOps delivery process, Maven commonly runs after source code is checked out from Git. The pipeline invokes Maven to compile the application, execute tests, package the output, and publish the resulting artifact to an approved artifact repository. Maven is therefore an important part of the build stage, but it does not replace source control, deployment automation, infrastructure provisioning, or runtime monitoring."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-1-section-2",
        "title": "Declarative Build Management and Convention over Configuration",
        "content": "Maven follows a declarative build approach. A project describes important information about itself and its build requirements, while Maven and its plugins provide the implementation that performs the required operations. This is different from writing a large collection of custom shell commands for every build activity. Developers define the project model and select the required build behavior instead of manually implementing every standard operation.\n\nMaven also uses convention over configuration. Standard directory locations, familiar project structure, and established lifecycle behavior allow many projects to build with relatively little custom configuration. For example, Java source files are conventionally stored under src/main/java, test source files under src/test/java, and generated build output under target. These conventions make projects easier for developers and automation systems to understand.\n\nConvention does not mean that every project must follow identical settings. Maven supports project-specific configuration when a genuine requirement exists. Teams should prefer standard behavior where possible and introduce customization only when it provides a clear technical benefit. Excessive custom build logic can make builds difficult to maintain, debug, and reproduce across developer machines and CI agents."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-1-section-3",
        "title": "Standard Maven Project Structure and Source Organization",
        "content": "A conventional Maven project separates application source code, test source code, resources, and generated build output. The directory src/main/java normally contains production Java classes. The directory src/main/resources contains resources required by the application, such as properties files, XML configuration, templates, and other files that must be included in the application output. The directory src/test/java contains automated test source code, while src/test/resources contains resources used by tests.\n\nMaven normally writes generated output to the target directory. Depending on the configured build, this directory can contain compiled classes, processed resources, test reports, packaged JAR or WAR files, and other intermediate results. The target directory is generally generated during the build and should not be treated as the authoritative source of application code.\n\nThis standard layout helps IDEs, developers, build plugins, and CI systems recognize project content without requiring every directory to be configured manually. A multi-module Maven project can also contain multiple module directories, each with its own project descriptor and source layout.\n\nUnderstanding this structure is important when diagnosing missing classes, resources that are not included in a package, tests that are not discovered, or generated files that unexpectedly appear in source control. Developers should investigate the source and resource directories separately from generated target output."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-1-section-4",
        "title": "Project Descriptor Fundamentals and Build Model",
        "content": "Maven uses a project descriptor named pom.xml to describe a project's identity, configuration, dependencies, and build-related requirements. POM stands for Project Object Model. Maven reads this descriptor to understand the project and construct the effective model used during a build. The POM is a central input to Maven rather than an executable script containing every build command.\n\nAt a fundamental level, the project descriptor communicates what the project is, which other components it requires, and how its build should be organized. Maven can also combine project-level configuration with inherited and externally supplied configuration when calculating the effective project model. This allows teams to share common build settings across related modules without copying every setting into every project.\n\nThe POM should be maintained as part of the project's source-controlled build configuration. A change to compiler settings, dependencies, packaging, or plugin configuration can change the resulting artifact even when the application source code itself has not changed.\n\nEngineers should review POM changes during code review and validate them through the normal build pipeline. A successful build should be based on the intended project descriptor and not on undocumented settings that exist only on one developer's machine."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-1-section-5",
        "title": "Artifact Identity and the Maven Project Ecosystem",
        "content": "Maven projects participate in an artifact ecosystem in which software components are identified, built, consumed, and distributed. A project normally produces an artifact such as a JAR, WAR, or another configured package. Other projects can consume that artifact as a dependency when its published identity and version are available to their build process.\n\nArtifact identity is important because a build system must distinguish one component from another and determine which version is required. Maven provides a structured coordinate system for identifying artifacts. The detailed coordinate elements and packaging choices belong to the dedicated Coordinates and Packaging navigation, but at the fundamentals level engineers should understand that an artifact is not identified safely by its filename alone.\n\nIn an enterprise DevOps workflow, a built artifact should be traceable to its source revision, build execution, version, and publication destination. A pipeline should not silently replace an existing release artifact with different content under the same release identity. This protects downstream environments from receiving unexpected binaries.\n\nTeams should establish clear versioning and release conventions and preserve build metadata. When a production deployment must be investigated, engineers should be able to identify exactly which artifact was promoted and which source revision produced it."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-1-section-6",
        "title": "External Libraries and Dependency-Based Application Development",
        "content": "Modern Java applications rarely contain every required capability in their own source code. They commonly use external libraries for logging, JSON processing, database access, HTTP communication, security, testing, and other functionality. Maven provides a standard mechanism for declaring these library requirements and obtaining the corresponding artifacts during a build.\n\nWithout a dependency management tool, developers may need to download JAR files manually, copy them into project directories, and coordinate versions across environments. This approach creates maintenance problems because different developers can accidentally use different library versions, required files can be omitted, and security updates become harder to track.\n\nMaven resolves declared dependencies through its configured repository system and makes the required artifacts available to the build. The resulting dependency information contributes to the classpath used by compilation and test execution. The exact rules for dependency declarations, version selection, scopes, exclusions, and transitive dependency behavior are covered in their respective dedicated navigations.\n\nFor a reliable project, dependencies should be intentionally selected, reviewed, and updated. Teams should avoid adding libraries without a clear requirement and should use dependency scanning and version review as part of their software supply-chain controls."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-1-section-7",
        "title": "Build Extensions and the Role of Maven Plugins",
        "content": "Maven uses plugins to perform many of the operations involved in a software build. Maven itself provides the project model and build execution framework, while plugins implement tasks such as compiling Java source code, running tests, packaging application files, generating reports, and checking project quality. This separation allows Maven to support different project requirements without embedding every possible build operation directly into its core.\n\nA plugin can provide one or more goals. A goal represents a specific operation that can be executed as part of a Maven build or invoked directly by a user. Maven's standard build behavior is associated with lifecycle execution, where configured plugin goals are bound to relevant lifecycle points. Developers can also invoke supported plugin goals directly when a task requires it.\n\nFor example, a Java project commonly uses compiler-related functionality to compile source code and test-related functionality to execute automated tests. Packaging functionality can assemble the final application artifact. The exact plugin configuration, goal names, version selection, and execution binding should be understood in the dedicated Maven Plugins navigation.\n\nFor production builds, teams should avoid relying on undocumented plugin defaults. Pin and review plugin versions where appropriate, understand which operations execute in the pipeline, and validate plugin changes before promoting the resulting artifact."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-1-section-8",
        "title": "Build Lifecycle as a Standardized Execution Model",
        "content": "Maven organizes common build activities through predefined lifecycles. A lifecycle represents an ordered model of build work, allowing developers and automation systems to request a standard level of build completion without manually listing every preceding operation. This makes Maven commands easier to use consistently across projects.\n\nFor example, a request to package a project normally requires earlier build work to be completed first. Maven uses the lifecycle structure to execute the applicable preceding steps before reaching the requested point. This provides a predictable sequence for activities such as validating project information, compiling source code, running tests, and assembling an artifact.\n\nMaven provides standard lifecycles for different purposes, including the commonly used default lifecycle for building and distributing project artifacts. The clean lifecycle handles removal of generated output, while the site lifecycle supports project documentation generation. The detailed phase order and the distinction between lifecycle phases and plugin goals are covered in the Build Lifecycle and Phases and Goals navigations.\n\nIn CI/CD, lifecycle-based commands help teams define consistent build stages. Engineers should understand what a selected command is expected to execute and should not assume that every Maven command performs a complete release or deployment. Build completion and application deployment are separate responsibilities."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-1-section-9",
        "title": "Local and Remote Artifact Repository Concepts",
        "content": "Maven uses repositories to obtain dependencies and make build artifacts available to other projects. A local repository is maintained on the developer or build-agent machine and stores artifacts that have already been downloaded or installed locally. Remote repositories provide artifacts over a network and may be operated by public hosting services or by an organization's internal artifact management platform.\n\nWhen a project requires a library, Maven checks the available repository information and retrieves the required artifact when it is not already available in the local repository. Reusing locally cached artifacts can reduce repeated downloads and improve build performance. However, a local cache should not be confused with an authoritative source for release artifacts.\n\nEnterprise teams commonly use internal repository managers to proxy approved public repositories, host private libraries, control access, and improve dependency availability. This gives organizations greater visibility into the software components used by their builds.\n\nRepository configuration, mirrors, authentication, repository policies, and publication behavior are addressed in the dedicated Repositories navigation. At the fundamentals level, engineers should understand that builds depend on artifact availability and network access, and that repository configuration is an important part of reliable and secure build infrastructure."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-1-section-10",
        "title": "Build Output, Packaging and Deliverable Artifacts",
        "content": "A successful Maven build can produce a deliverable artifact that is consumed by another system or deployed to an environment. The output depends on the project configuration and selected packaging type. Common Java deliverables include JAR files for libraries and executable applications, and WAR files for applications intended for compatible servlet containers. Other projects may produce different artifact formats through their configured build extensions and plugins.\n\nMaven normally creates generated build output inside the target directory. This can include compiled application classes, processed resources, test reports, and the final package. Developers should distinguish temporary build output from the source files and configuration required to reproduce that output.\n\nIn a CI/CD pipeline, the packaged artifact should be treated as a controlled release input. A pipeline can archive it, publish it to an artifact repository, scan it, attach metadata, and promote the same artifact through development, testing, staging, and production environments. Rebuilding independently for every environment can introduce differences and weaken traceability.\n\nA reliable build process records the artifact name, version, checksum or digest where applicable, source revision, and pipeline execution details. This evidence helps teams verify that the artifact deployed to production is the one that passed the required validation steps."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-1-section-11",
        "title": "Automated Testing and Build Quality Fundamentals",
        "content": "A software build should validate more than whether source code can be compiled. Automated tests help determine whether application behavior matches expected requirements. Maven projects commonly include unit tests and may also include integration tests, static analysis, code coverage, and other quality checks depending on the project's engineering standards.\n\nUnit tests generally validate small pieces of application behavior in isolation. Integration tests validate interactions between components or external systems. These test types may require different setup, execution rules, and environmental dependencies. A team should make clear which test groups run during normal development builds and which are reserved for dedicated pipeline stages.\n\nMaven can coordinate test execution through the build configuration and its test-related plugins. Test results and reports provide evidence that the build was validated. A pipeline should treat required test failures as meaningful failures rather than silently ignoring them to obtain a green build.\n\nFor production-quality delivery, teams should define mandatory checks, maintain reliable test data and environments, and investigate flaky tests. Skipping tests may be appropriate only under a controlled and documented exception process. A packaged artifact should not be promoted merely because the packaging command completed if required quality gates did not pass."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-1-section-12",
        "title": "Maven in Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery",
        "content": "Maven is frequently used as the build engine inside continuous integration and continuous delivery pipelines. After a source-control event triggers a pipeline, the CI agent checks out a particular revision and runs the project's Maven build. The pipeline can use Maven to validate the project, compile production code, execute tests, and create a package that is suitable for publication or later deployment.\n\nA reliable pipeline should use a controlled Java and Maven environment. It should obtain dependencies from approved repositories, provide required credentials securely, retain useful build logs, and publish outputs only after the required validation steps succeed. The pipeline should also record which source revision and build configuration produced the artifact.\n\nMaven commands should be consistent between local development and CI wherever practical. However, the CI environment must not depend on undocumented files or settings from a developer's workstation. Build agents should be provisioned or configured through repeatable mechanisms, and sensitive configuration should be supplied through the approved secret-management process.\n\nMaven performs build-related work; the CI/CD platform coordinates pipeline stages, approvals, artifact promotion, and deployment workflows. Keeping these responsibilities clear makes the delivery process easier to maintain and troubleshoot."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-1-section-13",
        "title": "Reproducible Builds, Supply-Chain Trust and Engineering Standards",
        "content": "A reproducible and trustworthy build should produce an expected result from a known source revision and a controlled set of build inputs. Build inputs include source code, project configuration, dependency versions, plugin versions, Java runtime details, repository contents, environment settings, and build commands. If these inputs change unexpectedly, the resulting artifact may also change.\n\nTeams should manage build configuration through version control, review dependency and plugin updates, and avoid relying on undocumented machine-specific settings. CI agents should use known tool versions and approved repository endpoints. Where practical, organizations can preserve build logs, artifact checksums, software bills of materials, and provenance metadata to improve traceability.\n\nSupply-chain security also requires attention to the libraries and plugins consumed by a project. A dependency or plugin can introduce vulnerabilities or unexpected behavior into the build. Organizations should use dependency scanning, vulnerability review, access controls, and approved publication policies as part of their development process.\n\nFor a production DevOps team, Maven fundamentals are not limited to running a successful command. Engineers should be able to explain how source code becomes a tested artifact, identify the inputs that influence the build, trace the artifact to its source revision, and demonstrate that the pipeline applies the organization's quality and security controls."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Maven Installation",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-2-section-1",
        "title": "Maven Installation Prerequisites and Environment Readiness",
        "content": "Before installing Apache Maven, prepare a compatible Java environment and confirm that the target machine can access the required installation files. Maven is a Java-based application, so it needs a supported Java runtime to start. For Java application development, a suitable JDK is normally required because the project may need Java compiler tools in addition to the runtime. The required Java version depends on the Maven release and the compatibility requirements of the project being built.\n\nCheck the operating system, processor architecture, available disk space, user permissions, and network restrictions before installation. The machine may be a Windows workstation, Linux server, macOS laptop, virtual machine, container image, or CI build agent. The installation method should match that environment.\n\nRun `java -version` to inspect the Java runtime currently selected by the shell. On Windows, `Get-Command java` or `where.exe java` can identify the executable being resolved. On Linux, `command -v java` and `which java` provide similar information. Confirm that the selected Java installation is the intended one rather than assuming that an installed JDK is automatically the active version.\n\nFor enterprise environments, check approved software sources and organizational security policies before downloading or installing tools. A controlled installation should use a known distribution, a documented version, and a repeatable configuration process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-2-section-2",
        "title": "Selecting and Obtaining the Official Apache Maven Distribution",
        "content": "Apache Maven is distributed as downloadable binary archives that contain the files required to run Maven. The official Apache Maven project distribution is the preferred source for obtaining a release. Select a stable version approved for the project or organization, and choose the archive format appropriate for the operating system. Windows users commonly use a ZIP archive, while Linux and macOS users commonly use a tar.gz archive.\n\nA binary distribution is intended for normal installation and execution. A source distribution contains the source code used to build Maven itself and is generally not required when the goal is simply to install Maven for application builds. Downloading the correct distribution avoids unnecessary compilation and reduces setup complexity.\n\nWhere the distribution site provides checksum or signature information, verify the downloaded file according to the published Apache verification instructions and the organization's security policy. A checksum can help detect accidental corruption, while signature verification can provide stronger evidence about the origin of the distribution when the signing key is trusted.\n\nDo not download Maven archives from unknown file-sharing websites or use an unverified executable bundled with unrelated software. Record the selected Maven version and download source so that workstation and CI-agent installations can be reproduced consistently."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-2-section-3",
        "title": "Installing Maven on Windows by Extracting the Binary Archive",
        "content": "On Windows, Maven can be installed by extracting the official binary ZIP archive into a stable directory. For example, an administrator or developer may extract a selected Maven release under `C:\\Tools\\Apache\\Maven`. The resulting directory should contain Maven folders such as `bin`, `boot`, and `conf`, along with Maven distribution files. The exact version directory name depends on the downloaded release and the chosen extraction location.\n\nAvoid extracting Maven into a temporary Downloads directory if the installation must remain available after cleanup. A stable location makes it easier to configure environment variables and update the installation later. If multiple versions are required for testing, keep them in separate versioned directories rather than overwriting one installation without recording the change.\n\nAfter extraction, locate the `bin` directory containing `mvn.cmd` and `mvn`. On Windows PowerShell, the command launcher is normally `mvn.cmd`. The Maven executable directory must be added to the command search path so that a new terminal can locate it without requiring the full file path.\n\nInstallation should be performed with permissions appropriate to the selected directory. A user-owned tools directory can avoid unnecessary administrator privileges for a personal development machine. For shared CI machines, follow the organization's managed software installation process and ensure that the build service account can read and execute the installation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-2-section-4",
        "title": "Configuring JAVA_HOME and Maven Environment Variables on Windows",
        "content": "Maven uses the Java environment selected by the operating system and shell. On Windows, `JAVA_HOME` should point to the root directory of the intended JDK installation, not directly to its `bin` directory and not to the `java.exe` file. For example, a JDK might be installed under `C:\\Program Files\\Java\\jdk-21`. The actual directory must match the JDK installed on the machine.\n\nThe Maven installation directory can be recorded in a variable such as `MAVEN_HOME`, pointing to the extracted Maven directory. `M2_HOME` may appear in older installation instructions, but it is not necessary for every modern Maven setup. The important requirement for normal command-line use is that the Maven `bin` directory is available through `PATH`.\n\nFor a temporary PowerShell session, an example configuration is:\n\n`$env:JAVA_HOME = \"C:\\Program Files\\Java\\jdk-21\"`\n\n`$env:MAVEN_HOME = \"C:\\Tools\\Apache\\Maven\\apache-maven-3.9.x\"`\n\n`$env:Path = \"$env:MAVEN_HOME\\bin;$env:JAVA_HOME\\bin;$env:Path\"`\n\nReplace these example directories with the actual installed locations. These assignments affect only the current PowerShell process and its child processes. Persistent user or machine environment settings should be configured through the approved Windows environment-variable interface or deployment process. Machine-level changes may require administrator permissions, and already-open terminals may need to be restarted to receive updated values."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-2-section-5",
        "title": "Installing Maven on Linux and macOS Systems",
        "content": "Linux and macOS systems can install Maven through an approved package manager or by extracting an official Apache Maven binary archive. Package managers simplify installation and updates, but the available Maven version depends on the operating system repository and package-manager configuration. A manually extracted distribution gives teams more direct control over the selected version, which can be useful when a project requires a specific release.\n\nFor a manual installation, extract the archive into a stable directory such as `/opt/apache-maven` on a Linux host or a managed tools directory on macOS. Configure `JAVA_HOME` to identify the intended JDK installation. Add the Maven `bin` directory to the shell's `PATH` so that the `mvn` command can be found. The exact Java installation path differs by operating system, JDK vendor, package manager, and installation method.\n\nFor a temporary shell session, a typical configuration pattern is:\n\n`export JAVA_HOME=\"/path/to/your/jdk\"`\n\n`export MAVEN_HOME=\"/opt/apache-maven\"`\n\n`export PATH=\"$MAVEN_HOME/bin:$JAVA_HOME/bin:$PATH\"`\n\nReplace the example paths with actual locations. For persistent configuration, use the appropriate shell startup file or managed environment configuration for the user's shell, such as a suitable profile file. After changing persistent settings, open a new terminal or reload the relevant shell configuration. Avoid assuming that every Linux distribution or macOS installation uses the same JDK directory."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-2-section-6",
        "title": "PATH Resolution and Maven Command Availability",
        "content": "The PATH environment variable contains directories that the operating system searches when a user enters a command without specifying its complete executable path. Adding Maven's `bin` directory to PATH allows a developer to run `mvn` from different project directories. Adding the JDK's `bin` directory also makes Java development tools available through commands such as `java` and `javac`.\n\nOn Windows, use `Get-Command mvn -All` or `where.exe mvn` to inspect which Maven command will be selected. Use `Get-Command java -All` or `where.exe java` to inspect Java command resolution. On Linux and macOS, `command -v mvn`, `which mvn`, and `command -v java` can help identify the selected executables.\n\nMultiple Maven or Java installations can exist on the same machine. If an older directory appears earlier in PATH, the shell may execute that version even after a newer release has been extracted. This can create confusing results when a developer believes the new installation is active. Check command resolution rather than relying only on the directory where the latest archive was extracted.\n\nAfter changing PATH in persistent settings, start a new terminal session and repeat the checks. A terminal that was opened before the change may still hold the previous environment. CI services and scheduled tasks may also use a different environment from an interactive desktop session, so verify the actual environment used by the build process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-2-section-7",
        "title": "Verifying Maven Installation with Version and Runtime Checks",
        "content": "The primary command for verifying a Maven installation is `mvn -version`. Run it from a new terminal after configuring the environment. A successful response normally displays the Maven version, Maven home directory, Java version, Java home directory, and operating-system information. These details help confirm both that the Maven launcher can be found and which Java installation Maven is using.\n\nA successful `mvn -version` command does not prove that every project will compile. It confirms that Maven can start with the selected Java runtime. A project may still require a different JDK release, a compiler toolchain, repository access, or additional configuration. Therefore, installation verification and project build verification are separate checks.\n\nUseful checks include `java -version`, `javac -version`, `mvn -version`, and command-resolution checks such as `Get-Command mvn` on PowerShell or `command -v mvn` on Linux and macOS. Compare the reported Maven home and Java home with the directories expected by the installation procedure.\n\nFor a stronger validation, create or use a small approved Maven project and execute a basic build from its project directory. Confirm that Maven can read the project descriptor, resolve required build components, and complete the requested operation. Keep the command output as evidence when preparing a development workstation or a new CI build agent."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-2-section-8",
        "title": "Java and Maven Version Compatibility",
        "content": "Maven runs on Java, so the Java runtime selected for Maven must be compatible with the installed Maven release. At the same time, the application being built may have its own Java language and runtime requirements. These are related but separate compatibility questions: one concerns the Java version used to start Maven, while the other concerns the Java version targeted by the project.\n\nA developer might run Maven using a newer JDK while compiling an application intended for an older Java release. This can be supported when the project is configured correctly and the required compiler toolchain is available. However, selecting a compiler release in project configuration does not automatically change the Java runtime used to launch Maven.\n\nCheck the official Maven release documentation and the project's supported Java requirements before selecting versions. Also inspect the CI agent's actual Java environment because a local build may succeed with a JDK that is not installed on the build server. A mismatch can cause startup errors, unsupported class-file versions, compiler failures, or inconsistent build results.\n\nFor controlled environments, document the Maven version, Java runtime used by Maven, and Java release targeted by the application. Use managed tool installations, containers, or explicit toolchain configuration when different projects require different Java versions. Do not upgrade a shared build agent without checking the compatibility impact on all projects using it."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-2-section-9",
        "title": "Maven Installation in CI Agents and Build Containers",
        "content": "A CI pipeline requires Maven to be available in the environment where build commands execute. There are several ways to provide it: install a managed Maven version on the build agent, use a CI platform's supported tool configuration, or use a container image that already contains the required Java and Maven tools. The correct choice depends on the CI platform, security requirements, agent lifecycle, and reproducibility goals.\n\nFor long-lived build agents, administrators should document the installation path, selected version, Java runtime, and environment configuration. For short-lived agents or container-based pipelines, defining the tool versions in a controlled image or provisioning definition can make the build environment easier to reproduce. Avoid depending on software that happens to be installed manually on one agent but is absent from other agents.\n\nThe pipeline should verify its toolchain before running the project build. A diagnostic step such as `mvn -version` records which Maven and Java versions are actually active. This is useful when a pipeline behaves differently from a developer workstation or another build agent.\n\nDo not place repository passwords, access tokens, or other secrets inside a Maven installation directory or a container image. Supply credentials through the CI platform's approved secret mechanism and restrict access to the build identity. Tool installation should be repeatable, while authentication and sensitive runtime configuration should remain separately controlled."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-2-section-10",
        "title": "Maven Settings File and User-Level Installation Configuration",
        "content": "Maven supports user-level and installation-level configuration through settings files. A common user settings file is located at `~/.m2/settings.xml` on Linux and macOS, and under the user's `.m2` directory on Windows. An installation can also include a global settings file inside its configuration directory. These files can influence repository access, mirrors, proxies, authentication references, and other environment-specific behavior.\n\nThe project POM and Maven settings file have different responsibilities. The POM describes the project and its build model, while settings are generally used for machine- or user-specific Maven environment configuration. This separation helps avoid placing personal credentials or workstation-specific repository details directly into a shared project descriptor.\n\nWhen a company uses an internal repository manager, the settings file may configure a mirror so that dependency requests are routed through an approved endpoint. Credentials should be referenced securely and supplied using the organization's secret-management approach. Avoid committing passwords or access tokens into source control, and do not copy another developer's personal settings file without reviewing its contents.\n\nFor troubleshooting, identify which settings file Maven is actually using and inspect the relevant configuration with care. A build can fail even when Maven is installed correctly if a proxy, mirror, repository credential, or network endpoint is misconfigured. Detailed repository behavior is covered in the dedicated Repositories navigation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-2-section-11",
        "title": "Updating, Switching and Maintaining Maven Installations",
        "content": "Maven installations should be maintained through a controlled version-management process. When a new release is required, obtain the approved distribution, verify it, extract it into a separate versioned directory, and test it before changing the active environment. Keeping the previous installation temporarily available provides a straightforward rollback path if the new version causes compatibility problems.\n\nOn developer machines, the active Maven version is normally determined by PATH and the configured installation directory. On CI agents, the active version may be selected through the CI platform's tool configuration, agent image, provisioning script, or pipeline definition. Updating one location does not guarantee that every build environment has been updated.\n\nBefore changing a shared installation, review project requirements and test representative builds. A newer Maven version may expose previously hidden configuration problems or interact differently with plugins and extensions. Do not assume that a successful version command guarantees compatibility with all existing projects.\n\nRecord the old and new versions, the reason for the update, validation evidence, and the rollback procedure. Remove obsolete installations only after confirming that no active project or pipeline still depends on them. A managed and documented installation process reduces unexpected differences between developer machines and production build infrastructure."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-2-section-12",
        "title": "Installation Validation Lab and Environment Readiness Checklist",
        "content": "Use this lab to verify a Maven installation on a development workstation or CI agent. First, identify the operating system and confirm that a supported Java installation is available. Run `java -version` and, when compiling Java source locally, `javac -version`. Record the selected Java version and confirm that JAVA_HOME points to the intended JDK root directory.\n\nNext, obtain an approved Apache Maven binary distribution and install it in a stable directory. Configure the Maven bin directory in PATH and open a fresh terminal session. Run `mvn -version` and verify that the output reports the expected Maven version, Maven home, Java version, and Java home. Use `Get-Command mvn` or `where.exe mvn` on Windows, or `command -v mvn` on Linux and macOS, to confirm executable resolution.\n\nFinally, run a basic build in an approved sample project or an existing project whose requirements are known. Confirm that Maven starts, reads the project descriptor, and completes the requested build operation. If dependency resolution fails, distinguish a tool installation problem from a repository, proxy, authentication, or project configuration problem.\n\nThe lab is complete when the active Maven and Java installations are identified, the environment paths are correct, the selected versions meet project requirements, and a controlled build succeeds. Record the operating system, Maven version, Java version, installation directory, verification output, and any environment-specific setup required to reproduce the installation."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Project Object Model",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-3-section-1",
        "title": "Project Object Model — Build Tool Fundamentals",
        "content": "# Project Object Model — Build Tool Fundamentals\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Project Object Model** in Stage 04: Docker Container Engineering.\n\nThis topic concerns Docker container engineering and its role in packaging, distributing, and operating applications. Docker uses images to define application files and runtime defaults, containers to execute isolated processes, networks to provide connectivity, and volumes to preserve data. The exact implementation must be understood in relation to the topic title, its configuration, host dependencies, security boundaries, and operational lifecycle.\n\nDefine the concept represented by this section and explain the engineering problem it solves. Describe where it appears in a Docker-based application lifecycle and identify the Docker components involved. Explain important terms in beginner-friendly language. Distinguish the responsibility of the Docker client, daemon, image, container, registry, network, or storage component wherever relevant.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Docker components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect the resulting state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Docker Architecture and Technical Behavior\n\nDocker provides a workflow for building, distributing, and running containerized applications. The Docker CLI communicates with the Docker Engine API. The daemon manages images, containers, networks, and volumes. Containers execute application processes with isolation features provided by the host operating system.\n\nAn image provides the filesystem and runtime configuration used to create a container. A container has its own runtime state and writable layer. Networks provide connectivity, and volumes or bind mounts provide access to persistent data. Registries distribute image content between development, CI, and deployment environments.\n\nFor **Project Object Model — Build Tool Fundamentals**, identify the relevant component, its inputs, its output, and the state it changes. Explain whether the operation happens during image construction, container startup, application execution, network communication, or data persistence. This distinction helps prevent confusion between an image configuration problem and a runtime failure.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Verify Docker CLI and Engine availability with docker version and docker info.\n2. Confirm the correct Docker context and target environment.\n3. Inspect the image, container, network, volume, or configuration involved.\n4. Review the required ports, filesystem paths, environment variables, permissions, and resource limits.\n5. Apply the Dockerfile, CLI, Compose, or runtime configuration change.\n6. Start or rebuild only the resources required for the operation.\n7. Inspect the resulting state and capture logs or command output.\n8. Run application-level validation rather than relying only on container status.\n9. Record the image tag or digest and relevant configuration for repeatability.\n\nDocker commands can affect host resources and persistent data. Verify the selected context and resource names before removing containers, networks, images, or volumes.\n\n## 4. Practical Docker Commands\n\nThe following examples demonstrate relevant inspection and operational commands. Replace example names and image references with values from the actual environment.\n\n`\bash id=\"dkr401\"\ndocker version\ndocker info\ndocker build --no-cache -t payments-api:1.0 .\ndocker image ls\ndocker image inspect payments-api:1.0\ndocker history payments-api:1.0\n`\n\nUse commands that inspect state before commands that modify or remove resources. Do not expose registry credentials, secret environment variables, or private configuration in terminal recordings and CI logs.\n\n## 5. Dockerfile Implementation Example\n\nA small Java application image can use a multi-stage build:\n\n`dockerfile id=\"dkr402\"\nFROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build\n\nWORKDIR /workspace\nCOPY pom.xml .\nCOPY src ./src\n\nRUN mvn -B clean package -DskipTests\n\nFROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nRUN useradd --system --uid 10001 appuser\n\nCOPY --from=build /workspace/target/*.jar app.jar\n\nUSER 10001\n\nEXPOSE 8080\n\nENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-jar\", \"/app/app.jar\"]\n`\n\nThe build stage contains the Maven toolchain and creates the application package. The runtime stage contains only the Java runtime and application artifact. This reduces unnecessary build tooling in the final image. A production implementation should pin approved base-image versions, scan the result, validate the JAR selection, and consider image provenance and patching requirements.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment service that must run consistently on developer machines, CI agents, and production hosts. The team creates a Dockerfile that builds the application and packages its runtime dependencies into an image.\n\nA Jenkins pipeline checks out a specific source commit, builds the image, runs automated tests, and performs a vulnerability scan. After successful validation, the pipeline publishes the image to a controlled registry. The image is identified by a versioned tag and immutable digest.\n\nThe deployment process retrieves the approved image and starts the service with environment-specific configuration. Persistent transaction data is stored outside the disposable container layer. Network rules expose only the required application ports, and the container runs with restricted privileges.\n\nDuring a production incident, engineers inspect the container state, logs, resource consumption, network connectivity, and image digest. They can identify the exact image version and source revision instead of guessing which build is running.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Using an unpinned base image can introduce unexpected changes during rebuilds.\n- Copying the entire repository into the build context can increase build time and expose unnecessary files.\n- Embedding credentials in Dockerfile instructions can leave secrets in image layers.\n- Running every application process as root increases the impact of a container compromise.\n- Storing database files only in a container writable layer risks data loss.\n- Publishing the wrong host port can make a healthy application unreachable.\n- Assuming container startup means application readiness can cause dependent services to fail.\n- Using mutable image tags without recording digests weakens deployment traceability.\n- Ignoring resource limits can allow one container to exhaust host capacity.\n- Removing volumes during cleanup can permanently delete persistent application data.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by identifying the affected resource and capturing its current state. Use docker ps -a to inspect container status, docker logs to inspect application output, and docker inspect to examine configuration and runtime state.\n\nFor image-build failures, review the Dockerfile instruction, build context, .dockerignore rules, base image, and build output. For container startup failures, inspect the entrypoint, command, permissions, environment, and exit code. For connectivity failures, check port publishing, network membership, DNS, routing, and host firewall rules. For storage failures, inspect mounts, ownership, disk capacity, and volume state.\n\nCheck daemon and host logs when container-level evidence does not explain the issue. Preserve logs and important data before removing or recreating resources. After applying a correction, verify both container health and actual application behavior.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse trusted and maintained base images. Pin versions and record image digests for production releases. Scan images for known vulnerabilities and establish a patching process. Avoid embedding secrets in images or source repositories.\n\nRun application processes as non-root users where possible. Drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, avoid privileged containers, restrict host mounts, and protect access to the Docker daemon socket. Apply network segmentation and expose only required ports.\n\nUse volumes or external storage for important data. Configure resource limits, health checks, logging, and monitoring. Keep development, CI, and production configuration separate. Promote the same tested image between environments rather than rebuilding different images for each deployment target.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Project Object Model — Build Tool Fundamentals** is complete only when the expected Docker state and application behavior are verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The correct Docker context and host were selected.\n- The intended image, container, network, or volume was used.\n- Configuration values and runtime permissions are correct.\n- The expected container process is running.\n- Application logs do not show unresolved startup failures.\n- Required network paths and service endpoints work.\n- Persistent data remains available after container recreation where required.\n- Resource limits and security settings match the intended policy.\n- The image tag or digest can be traced to the validated build.\n- The operation can be repeated using documented steps.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a small application image using a Dockerfile. Build the image with a versioned tag and run it as a container with an explicitly published application port.\n\nInspect the image and container configuration. Read application logs and verify the service endpoint. Introduce one controlled problem, such as an incorrect port mapping or invalid startup command. Observe the failure and collect evidence using Docker inspection and logs.\n\nCorrect the configuration and recreate the container. Confirm that the application works and explain which configuration change resolved the issue. If the application requires persistent data, verify that the data remains available after replacing the container.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Project Object Model — Build Tool Fundamentals** means, why Docker uses the relevant capability, which components participate, how the operation works, and how the result is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the initial state, the exact Docker configuration or commands, the resulting image or container behavior, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply listing commands. Explain what each command changes, which component performs the work, and how the final application behavior is confirmed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-3-section-2",
        "title": "Project Object Model — Project Configuration and Structure",
        "content": "# Project Object Model — Project Configuration and Structure\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Project Object Model** in Stage 04: Docker Container Engineering.\n\nThis topic concerns Docker container engineering and its role in packaging, distributing, and operating applications. Docker uses images to define application files and runtime defaults, containers to execute isolated processes, networks to provide connectivity, and volumes to preserve data. The exact implementation must be understood in relation to the topic title, its configuration, host dependencies, security boundaries, and operational lifecycle.\n\nDescribe prerequisites and the installation or configuration sequence. Cover supported operating-system requirements, Docker Engine or Docker Desktop differences, service startup, CLI connectivity, user permissions, storage configuration, and network access. Explain how to verify installed versions and daemon health. Include safe upgrade planning, package compatibility, service restart impact, and backup considerations for important Docker data.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Docker components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect the resulting state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Docker Architecture and Technical Behavior\n\nDocker provides a workflow for building, distributing, and running containerized applications. The Docker CLI communicates with the Docker Engine API. The daemon manages images, containers, networks, and volumes. Containers execute application processes with isolation features provided by the host operating system.\n\nAn image provides the filesystem and runtime configuration used to create a container. A container has its own runtime state and writable layer. Networks provide connectivity, and volumes or bind mounts provide access to persistent data. Registries distribute image content between development, CI, and deployment environments.\n\nFor **Project Object Model — Project Configuration and Structure**, identify the relevant component, its inputs, its output, and the state it changes. Explain whether the operation happens during image construction, container startup, application execution, network communication, or data persistence. This distinction helps prevent confusion between an image configuration problem and a runtime failure.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Verify Docker CLI and Engine availability with docker version and docker info.\n2. Confirm the correct Docker context and target environment.\n3. Inspect the image, container, network, volume, or configuration involved.\n4. Review the required ports, filesystem paths, environment variables, permissions, and resource limits.\n5. Apply the Dockerfile, CLI, Compose, or runtime configuration change.\n6. Start or rebuild only the resources required for the operation.\n7. Inspect the resulting state and capture logs or command output.\n8. Run application-level validation rather than relying only on container status.\n9. Record the image tag or digest and relevant configuration for repeatability.\n\nDocker commands can affect host resources and persistent data. Verify the selected context and resource names before removing containers, networks, images, or volumes.\n\n## 4. Practical Docker Commands\n\nThe following examples demonstrate relevant inspection and operational commands. Replace example names and image references with values from the actual environment.\n\n`\bash id=\"dkr401\"\ndocker version\ndocker info\ndocker ps -a\ndocker image ls\ndocker network ls\ndocker volume ls\ndocker system df\n`\n\nUse commands that inspect state before commands that modify or remove resources. Do not expose registry credentials, secret environment variables, or private configuration in terminal recordings and CI logs.\n\n## 5. Dockerfile Implementation Example\n\nA small Java application image can use a multi-stage build:\n\n`dockerfile id=\"dkr402\"\nFROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build\n\nWORKDIR /workspace\nCOPY pom.xml .\nCOPY src ./src\n\nRUN mvn -B clean package -DskipTests\n\nFROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nRUN useradd --system --uid 10001 appuser\n\nCOPY --from=build /workspace/target/*.jar app.jar\n\nUSER 10001\n\nEXPOSE 8080\n\nENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-jar\", \"/app/app.jar\"]\n`\n\nThe build stage contains the Maven toolchain and creates the application package. The runtime stage contains only the Java runtime and application artifact. This reduces unnecessary build tooling in the final image. A production implementation should pin approved base-image versions, scan the result, validate the JAR selection, and consider image provenance and patching requirements.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment service that must run consistently on developer machines, CI agents, and production hosts. The team creates a Dockerfile that builds the application and packages its runtime dependencies into an image.\n\nA Jenkins pipeline checks out a specific source commit, builds the image, runs automated tests, and performs a vulnerability scan. After successful validation, the pipeline publishes the image to a controlled registry. The image is identified by a versioned tag and immutable digest.\n\nThe deployment process retrieves the approved image and starts the service with environment-specific configuration. Persistent transaction data is stored outside the disposable container layer. Network rules expose only the required application ports, and the container runs with restricted privileges.\n\nDuring a production incident, engineers inspect the container state, logs, resource consumption, network connectivity, and image digest. They can identify the exact image version and source revision instead of guessing which build is running.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Using an unpinned base image can introduce unexpected changes during rebuilds.\n- Copying the entire repository into the build context can increase build time and expose unnecessary files.\n- Embedding credentials in Dockerfile instructions can leave secrets in image layers.\n- Running every application process as root increases the impact of a container compromise.\n- Storing database files only in a container writable layer risks data loss.\n- Publishing the wrong host port can make a healthy application unreachable.\n- Assuming container startup means application readiness can cause dependent services to fail.\n- Using mutable image tags without recording digests weakens deployment traceability.\n- Ignoring resource limits can allow one container to exhaust host capacity.\n- Removing volumes during cleanup can permanently delete persistent application data.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by identifying the affected resource and capturing its current state. Use docker ps -a to inspect container status, docker logs to inspect application output, and docker inspect to examine configuration and runtime state.\n\nFor image-build failures, review the Dockerfile instruction, build context, .dockerignore rules, base image, and build output. For container startup failures, inspect the entrypoint, command, permissions, environment, and exit code. For connectivity failures, check port publishing, network membership, DNS, routing, and host firewall rules. For storage failures, inspect mounts, ownership, disk capacity, and volume state.\n\nCheck daemon and host logs when container-level evidence does not explain the issue. Preserve logs and important data before removing or recreating resources. After applying a correction, verify both container health and actual application behavior.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse trusted and maintained base images. Pin versions and record image digests for production releases. Scan images for known vulnerabilities and establish a patching process. Avoid embedding secrets in images or source repositories.\n\nRun application processes as non-root users where possible. Drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, avoid privileged containers, restrict host mounts, and protect access to the Docker daemon socket. Apply network segmentation and expose only required ports.\n\nUse volumes or external storage for important data. Configure resource limits, health checks, logging, and monitoring. Keep development, CI, and production configuration separate. Promote the same tested image between environments rather than rebuilding different images for each deployment target.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Project Object Model — Project Configuration and Structure** is complete only when the expected Docker state and application behavior are verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The correct Docker context and host were selected.\n- The intended image, container, network, or volume was used.\n- Configuration values and runtime permissions are correct.\n- The expected container process is running.\n- Application logs do not show unresolved startup failures.\n- Required network paths and service endpoints work.\n- Persistent data remains available after container recreation where required.\n- Resource limits and security settings match the intended policy.\n- The image tag or digest can be traced to the validated build.\n- The operation can be repeated using documented steps.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a small application image using a Dockerfile. Build the image with a versioned tag and run it as a container with an explicitly published application port.\n\nInspect the image and container configuration. Read application logs and verify the service endpoint. Introduce one controlled problem, such as an incorrect port mapping or invalid startup command. Observe the failure and collect evidence using Docker inspection and logs.\n\nCorrect the configuration and recreate the container. Confirm that the application works and explain which configuration change resolved the issue. If the application requires persistent data, verify that the data remains available after replacing the container.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Project Object Model — Project Configuration and Structure** means, why Docker uses the relevant capability, which components participate, how the operation works, and how the result is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the initial state, the exact Docker configuration or commands, the resulting image or container behavior, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply listing commands. Explain what each command changes, which component performs the work, and how the final application behavior is confirmed."
